WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.000
sulfates fatty alcohols silicones preservatives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.600
why are they in shampoo and conditioner&nbsp;
and do you need to avoid them

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.280
hi i'm michelle chemistry phd and&nbsp;
cosmetic chemist today we're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.800
a deep dive into some of the key&nbsp;
ingredients in hair care products

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.480
this video was sponsored by the ordinary&nbsp;
who turned a lot of people into skincare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.560
nerds with their focus on ingredients&nbsp;
and now they're moving into hair care

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.160
let's start with shampoo

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.520
to me the best way to approach&nbsp;
analyzing formulas is to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.800
what is the point of this&nbsp;
product what is its function

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.880
shampoo obviously cleans your hair&nbsp;
and scalp just like skin your scalp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.600
produces sebum which we usually think of as oil

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.000
this oil keeps your skin and scalp nice and&nbsp;
soft and it also travels along the hair to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.160
keep that soft as well but sometimes we have&nbsp;
too much oil some of us like me more than others

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.160
it also tends to stick to things like&nbsp;
dirt dust skin flakes and pollution

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.120
you might also use styling&nbsp;
products that need to be washed off

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.840
the problem is if you just try to use&nbsp;
water to degreasify everything a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.760
it usually doesn't work very well because&nbsp;
oil and water don't mix so to make them mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.960
we use a shampoo and the key ingredients&nbsp;
in the shampoo that clean are surfactants

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.400
surfactants are really useful ingredients because&nbsp;
they have a head that likes water and a tail that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.720
likes oil and so instead of repelling each other&nbsp;
the surfactants allow the oil to basically be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.320
smuggled into the water in lots of tiny droplets&nbsp;
and then you can wash that away down the drain

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.360
there are surfactants in tons of&nbsp;
different products because getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.720
oil and water to stay together is really useful

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:41.040
for cleaning the best surfactants are anionic&nbsp;
surfactants these have a negatively charged head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.960
and they're used in tons of cleaning&nbsp;
products shampoo obviously but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.600
laundry detergent dishwashing detergent&nbsp;
body washes and face cleansers

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.240
some of the anionic surfactants&nbsp;
that you might have seen in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.600
shampoos and cleansers are sulfates&nbsp;
sarcosinates taurates and isethionates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.840
traditional soaps also have anionic&nbsp;
surfactants called carboxylates

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.880
so that's what the sulfates are doing but&nbsp;
why are people talking about sulfate-free

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.720
well a lot of it is based on misinformation once&nbsp;
you start digging into the science behind shampoos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.800
you start to realize that limiting yourself to&nbsp;
sulfate-free shampoos doesn't really make sense

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.280
sulfate surfactants are a diverse group of&nbsp;
ingredients that behave in different ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.240
and on top of that they can be&nbsp;
combined in formulas in lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.040
of different ways to give very different shampoos

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.960
it's a bit like eggs in a recipe just&nbsp;
seeing eggs in the ingredients list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.480
doesn't tell you whether they used&nbsp;
the whites or the yolks or a mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.800
how the food tastes or whether you'll&nbsp;
like it unless you have an egg allergy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.520
in which case you probably should avoid it&nbsp;
unless you really like pain or you're vegan

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.840
so generalizing sulfates in products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.920
doesn't really work it isn't like&nbsp;
shampoos with sulfates are always harsher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:58.400
or more irritating or strip colour faster than&nbsp;
sulfate-free shampoos there is a lot of overlap

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.800
sulfates are some of the most commonly used&nbsp;
anionic surfactants in personal care products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.360
the two you'll see most in shampoos&nbsp;
are sodium lauryl sulfate sls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.920
and sodium laureth sulfate sles these sound&nbsp;
pretty similar but they're actually very different

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.320
sodium lauryl sulfate is the more&nbsp;
famous one and it has a bad reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.480
which has now spread to other&nbsp;
sulfates it has a narrow structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.160
and that means it can potentially absorb into&nbsp;
skin more easily and keep irritating for longer

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.880
if sls is in a formula on its own in a&nbsp;
high concentration then it will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.320
be quite irritating and this is one of the big&nbsp;
reasons why it has this irritant reputation

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.640
a lot of the time in peer-reviewed&nbsp;
studies and in dermatology patch testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:48.240
sls is used by itself to make sure it is the&nbsp;
specific ingredient that someone is reacting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.840
but this also means it isn't in&nbsp;
its usual environment which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.200
in a product with a lot of other ingredients that&nbsp;
change how it interacts with skin and with hair

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.920
there are lots of formulating tricks that are&nbsp;
usually used in sls products here are some of them

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.320
creating mixed micelles by combining sls with&nbsp;
other surfactants this makes sls less likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.760
wander off on its own into your skin or hair&nbsp;
it's like peer pressure but for molecules

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.480
adding polymers that act as a raft&nbsp;
to hold onto free sls molecules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.600
again this is so it can't run&nbsp;
away into your skin or hair

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.280
adding skin protecting ingredients&nbsp;
like humectants and antioxidants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.640
that can prevent or reverse some of the irritation

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:35.600
now sles sodium laureth sulfate actually&nbsp;
isn't one single ingredient it's actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.560
category of ingredients that are all listed&nbsp;
under the same name in an ingredients list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.360
ingredient lists don't really lie but they&nbsp;
don't really tell you the full story either

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.840
sles is ethoxylated which means it's sls but&nbsp;
with a bunch of ethoxy units inserted next to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:58.400
sulfur like this the number of units can change&nbsp;
and this changes how the specific type of sles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.440
interacts with everything else water&nbsp;
oil or other surfactant molecules

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.040
having these ethoxy units means that less&nbsp;
sles will wander off on its own into your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.280
compared to sls ethoxylation&nbsp;
also makes the molecule bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.280
which means it doesn't penetrate&nbsp;
skin as easily and is more gentle

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.360
it's a bit like walking through a crowd if&nbsp;
you want to get through a crowd faster you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.880
try to hunch and make yourself&nbsp;
smaller you don't spread your arms out

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.000
there's one study that found that sles was&nbsp;
gentler than sls which is not very surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.080
but it was also gentler than a whole bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.160
of non-sulfate surfactants that you&nbsp;
often find in sulfate-free products

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.960
and on top of this formulators&nbsp;
can use the same formulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.280
tricks that i mentioned before to&nbsp;
make sles products even gentler

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.720
now with color fading i have a strong personal&nbsp;
interest in this as you can probably tell

