00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.440
now onto hair conditioners hair&nbsp;
conditioners have a few more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.360
functions than shampoo and different&nbsp;
people use them for different purposes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.560
if you have short hair you might&nbsp;
not actually need hair conditioner

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.880
conditioners can add shine smooth&nbsp;
over damage and reduce tangling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:51.760
and static to do this they leave a&nbsp;
very thin coating on hair strands

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.880
there is this myth that cheap&nbsp;
conditioners will coat hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.680
but expensive ones don't this is not&nbsp;
true you need a coating because your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:05.040
hair is dead and as it grows it accumulates&nbsp;
damage and ends up looking chipped like this

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.560
hair conditioner smooth over this&nbsp;
roughness by plugging in the gaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.240
so hair doesn't snag and cause&nbsp;
more of these bits to break off

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:17.120
the main ingredients used in hair conditioners&nbsp;
are cationic surfactants these are a lot like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.400
the anionic surfactants we saw in shampoos&nbsp;
except they have a positively charged head

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.800
when hair is wet it gets a negative charge&nbsp;
and the damaged bits of hair usually have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:31.200
a stronger negative charge opposite&nbsp;
charges attract and so these positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.760
heads in the cationic surfactants help&nbsp;
them stick to the negatively charged hair

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:41.040
the positive charges are also handy because&nbsp;
once you have enough attached the hair becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:46.320
slightly positive which will actually repel&nbsp;
extra cationic surfactants what this means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.520
is you end up with a thinner more even film&nbsp;
that feels silky rather than the sort of film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:57.120
that you might get with a lot of oils like your&nbsp;
natural hair oil that's heavy and weighs hair down

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.720
the most common type of cationic&nbsp;
surfactant that you'll see in conditioners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:07.680
are quaternary ammonium ions nicknamed quats&nbsp;
you'll see "-onium" in a lot of their names

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.600
the ordinary's conditioner has two&nbsp;
percent behentrimonium chloride

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:16.400
in conditioners these cationic surfactants&nbsp;
are usually combined with fatty alcohols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:21.440
the most common fatty alcohol is cetearyl&nbsp;
alcohol which is a mix of cetyl and stearyl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:26.480
alcohol these fatty alcohols make the conditioner&nbsp;
thicker so it's easier to apply in your hair

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.160
more importantly it also combines with&nbsp;
these cationic surfactants to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:34.080
an ordered arrangement called a&nbsp;
lamellar structure this is a bit like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.240
a pile of flat tiles that can slide&nbsp;
over each other and coat the hair

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:41.520
these main ingredients are also why&nbsp;
a lot of people get breakouts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:45.280
their back with conditioner cationic&nbsp;
surfactants are quite irritating and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:48.880
fatty alcohols are pretty good at&nbsp;
clogging pores so if you find you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:53.200
get a lot of back acne make sure you scrub&nbsp;
